{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/785934-reality-enforcement-and-ripple","id":785934,"title":"Reality Enforcement and Ripple","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-12-14T06:25:49-08:00","id":785934,"title":"Reality Enforcement and Ripple","content":"Ok, so two questions. Reality Enforcement (the Exploit) has no duration. Is it meant to last a scene, a number of turns equal to successes, or what?<br /> <br /> On a similar level, Ripple is apparently a Reflexive Embed that affects the entire scene but only targets one individual, is reflexive to activate, has no way to contest it, and adds 50% of the damage dealt to the target on top of the damage just inflicted. Correct? Some of the language really makes it sound like it was meant to impact everyone in the scene equally, and the lack of any contested roll or penalty sort of suggests that as well. But as written, it's a Reflexive power that seems to last the rest of the scene. It can add a lot of damage to the target and the only reason it isn't going to end up on every single enemy within 1-2 turns of the combat beginning would be due to the GM mandating Compromise checks for Embed abuse. In which case, how many activations before you draw the line? It kind of stands out as a direct combat Embed, since Shift Consequences and the like force Compromise with every attempt <i>and</i> are penalized by the target's Resistance traits, while others like Knockout Punch have subsequently been ruled not to effect Angels and other such supernatural powers. A no cost, almost guaranteed success power that doesn't even touch the action economy seems disproportionately power by comparison, and the messy way it's written has my group wondering if something is missing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T09:44:01-08:00","id":786041,"title":"","content":"I assume Reality Enforcement is for a scene. It says a short time, so its not an indefinite thing. Generally speaking, if you're using Reality Enforcement, angels are already there, or are on their way, since that's a pretty big flag. You won't want to stick around in that area for long, so I'm not sure the exact time it lasts is relevant. Scenes end when everyone leaves the area. If you absolutely need a time frame, I'd say that 1 minute per dot of Primum is good. <br /> <br /> Ripple is a contested power that affects everyone within the scene, for the duration of the scene. You activate it once, and everything goes crazy. Remember, though, this affects you and your allies as much as it does your enemies, so its a double edged sword that can be as disasterous for the ring as it is beneficial. That's the real kicker, and why its so deadly. The fact it can bite you in the arse. Sure, you can Just Bruise the extra damage, but then you are starting to be more and more inching towards Compromise checks for being improbable.<br /> <br /> How often should you allow Embed abuse before the G-M catches on and starts Compromise checks? That depends on how improbable things appear. That's up to the ST when things start to stretch credibility.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-14T10:02:30-08:00","id":786055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/785934-reality-enforcement-and-ripple?p=786041#post786041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I assume Reality Enforcement is for a scene. It says a short time, so its not an indefinite thing. Generally speaking, if you're using Reality Enforcement, angels are already there, or are on their way, since that's a pretty big flag. You won't want to stick around in that area for long, so I'm not sure the exact time it lasts is relevant. Scenes end when everyone leaves the area. If you absolutely need a time frame, I'd say that 1 minute per dot of Primum is good.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, if we're assuming a scene, sure. The question was mostly whether or not it would be limited to a number of turns (turns equal to Primum, turns equal to successes, something like that) or whether it would be safe to assume that it lasts the duration of the fight.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/785934-reality-enforcement-and-ripple?p=786041#post786041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ripple is a contested power that affects everyone within the scene, for the duration of the scene. You activate it once, and everything goes crazy. Remember, though, this affects you and your allies as much as it does your enemies, so its a double edged sword that can be as disasterous for the ring as it is beneficial. That's the real kicker, and why its so deadly. The fact it can bite you in the arse. Sure, you can Just Bruise the extra damage, but then you are starting to be more and more inching towards Compromise checks for being improbable.<br /> <br /> How often should you allow Embed abuse before the G-M catches on and starts Compromise checks? That depends on how improbable things appear. That's up to the ST when things start to stretch credibility.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flowers of Hell, page 96</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If successful, the entire scene is affected. Any harm caused will be followed with a relative amount of injury soon thereaf- ter. While this secondary injury isn\u2019t necessarily as bad, it can turn a bad situation disastrous. <b>This Embed affects a single target with each use.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Your interpretation is exactly the same as mine the first time I read it, and the source of the group's confusion. Ripple reads a lot like it was originally written as an &quot;effects the entire scene&quot; power, then rejigged into a single target power. But because of its nature as a Reflexive action with no way to contest or resist it, it's trivial to apply it to every enemy in a scene with the only possible limiting factor being &quot;the GM calls for Compromise checks&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-15T03:55:48-08:00","id":786629,"title":"","content":"It feels that the intention is that it lasts for the duration of the battle. <br /> <br /> And, huh, I missed the bit about single target. Ah, well. *shrugs* I'm going with my original statement on it being based on context. Embeds causing Compromise has a lot in common with the old idea of Improbable magic, ala Mage. It all depends on when an observer starts considering what's going on isn't a coincidence, and starts becoming a sign of the impossible. Its an entirely subjective thing.<br /> <br /> As for the power level.... Still not too bad of a power. The vast majority of enemies a demon fights are either 1) other demons, 2) angels, or 3) mortals. Mortals are easy to murder, demons have lots of anti-damage techniques (armor, just bruised, etc) that make Ripple far less effective, and angels are powerhouses that require every advantage possible to just survive, and filling the health track with damage doesn't even kill them, just sends them away for a bit. Demon is a spy game - if you can stack things in your favor, you'll win with the assassination strike. <br /> <br /> That, however, draws attention. And the attention is bad for characters. Doesn't even need to be a Compromise check - someone dies from Ripple, the police come and investigate. They hear about one of your Covers that attacked before the freaky stuff happened, they start looking into you. That is its own form of bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}